## Codex Tool Usage

- Pass an explicit `workdir` to shell command tools.
- Use `rtk` for token-heavy shell output when it is available and supports the command: `rtk git status`, `rtk git diff`, `rtk rg`, `rtk pytest -q`, `rtk cargo test`.
- Use `rtk proxy` only when raw output is required.
- For unsupported syntax, use the native command; do not force an incompatible rewrite.
- A `PreToolUse` hook denies `cat` of files over 350 lines. Read a bounded range with `sed -n`, or delegate the reading with `bulk-read` as described in the `my-bulk-read` skill.
- Use skills for occasional multi-step workflows so their detailed instructions load only when relevant.

## Codex Browser Routing

- Use web search or a purpose-built connector for public, read-only research that does not require rendered or interactive page state.
- When a safely resolved Orca CLI is available in an Orca-managed session, use it and Orca's embedded browser for browser UI automation. Use the `Work` browser profile only for approved work sites that require its imported session. Resolve its current ID with `orca tab profile list --json`; do not hardcode the ID or pass the label to `--profile`.
- Do not use the `Work` profile for unrelated or untrusted sites. Treat its cookies and authenticated session as credentials, and never print cookie values or request, copy, or expose passwords.
- Use Chrome or Computer Use only when the user explicitly requests it or the task requires a live Chrome tab, extension, password manager, passkey, or other state unavailable in Orca's browser.
- Do not silently switch from an available, selected Orca browser to Chrome, Computer Use, or standalone `agent-browser`. If the `Work` profile is missing, authentication has expired, or Orca cannot complete the task, stop and report the specific blocker.
- Outside an Orca-managed session, or when no safe Orca CLI executable is installed, use standalone `agent-browser` for browser UI automation when it is available. On Linux, never probe or invoke bare `orca`; follow the `orca-cli` skill's executable selection rules.

## Codex Subagent Routing

- Use subagents for independent work when parallel execution can reduce completion time after dispatch and review overhead. Read the `my-subagent` skill for task selection, dispatch, and verification.
- Always set `fork_turns` explicitly when calling `spawn_agent`. Default to `"none"`; follow the `my-subagent` skill's context-selection rules before inheriting any conversation turns.
- Every time you call `wait_agent`, explicitly set `timeout_ms` to twice the estimated remaining completion time in milliseconds, within the tool's minimum and maximum limits. If you cannot estimate it, explicitly use the configured default of `120000` ms. Completion notifications end the wait early, so do not shorten it for status polling. After a timeout, update the estimate and apply the same rule.
